A||N||9||TheFiero64||!!EVERY FORUM MEMBER MUST READ!!||||4||
Z||000000||TheFiero64||08-27-2000||02:48 PM||||I KNOW IT IS LONG BUT <B>DON"T PASS THIS OVER PLEASE</B> <I>sorry for my shout</I><P>Every one that knows Bob Parker at kickhill knows he is the nicest guy ever. He has had a fire this week and NEFA wants to help him out. Please help us to ease his pain in this time of loss.<P><B>Read this from ray paulk</B><BR>As all or most of you know, the garage at Bob Parker's Kick Hill Farm burned down last Wednesday evening/Thursday morning. Fortunately it was not his barn with all the Fiero parts but it was his sole work area<BR>where all those parts were separated from their unit. To a Fiero lover, this almost sounds like sacrilege but as Bob puts it, every car I part out helps others stay on the road. Ya know, he's right.<P>After yesterdays Swap Meet, on the ride home I was trying to think of some gift we could give Bob and Holly as a thank-you for their<BR>hospitality... Normally this would be like a gift certificate for "Uncle D's" restaurant where many of us ate dinner last evening. But this just seemed kind of empty and trite considering what they've been through and<BR>the current situation.<P>Then it dawned on me. Bob was telling us what he needed all day, we just didn't listen. All day he apologized that he couldn't loan us any tools to take remove parts 'cause all his tools were in the garage and were destroyed in the fire. It was like "dawn shines on Marblehead",<BR>shoot.... Bob helps us out by supplying parts. Although its a portion of his livelihood, he always goes the extra mile for us. So here's the thought:<P>Anyone who wants to chip in and we'll buy Bob a set of tools. Craftsman has a wonderful set of auto mechanic tools for about $300. Or we could get him an air compressor and air tools if we raise enough. I just went through a search for a compressor and settled on a new Craftsman 60 gal vertical with a direct drive motor. It is awesome and I'd highly recommend it to anyone. This would go about $400-450 for the compressor, $200 for the filter, regulator and lubricator assembly (trust me I just went through it) plus hose, fittings and a few tools. Maybe that's a bit too much but its an idea.<P>I'm starting with a personal donation of $20. If you want to be part of this, please send your donation to me by next Friday. If a check, make it out to me, Raymond C. Paulk. Please email me to let me know something's coming. On Saturday we'll get something for Bob with the funds collected. Any funds after that will be presented to Bob (and Holly) as a gift certificate.<P>Please send donations to:<P>Kick Hill<BR>c/o Ray Paulk<BR>19 Ben's Way<BR>Hopedale, MA  01747<P>We'll get him something from all of us, even if its $20 worth.<P>Let me know if you have any other ideas.<P>Ray Paulk<BR>raypaulk@fierottop.com<BR>New England Fiero Association, President<BR>Mid-Atlantic Fiero Owners Association, Director<P><B>From Eric Schneck</B><BR>As a foot note to Ray's note (below), I just want to touch on the scope of things lost in the fire. I spoke with both Bob & Holly at different intervals during the day, and each spoke about what was lost in the fire. Bob spoke about his '62 Impala with the '94 (??) Cadillac Northstar and running gear that he was building for himself. Parts of his Beechwood airplane that he was trying to sell were destroyed, thus depreciating the value of the plane by about $3000. He spoke of the Huey helicopters as if they were human, saying that "it was a shame that they<BR>made it through the (Vietnam) war, only to have this happen to them". And lastly, Holly spoke about the tools. These weren't just any tools, mind you. Bob's father had helped him build his first tool box and stocked it with his own passed down hand tools. And Bob had just helped his son with *his* first tool box. Both were lost in the fire. The<BR>memories that went up in flames in that fire will be gone, but new ones can be reinstated from the ashes by us, Bob's "second family".<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||4
Z||000001||Patrick||08-27-2000||03:22 PM||||<BR>I'm not trying to be a jerk here, so please don't accuse me of being cold and/or callous, but didn't Bob Parker have any <I>insurance</I>? My heart goes out to the guy for having to go through something traumatic like this, but it just seems strange to me that a business would not have adequate insurance coverage. Everyone (including home owners and tenants) should have proper fire and theft coverage. None of us enjoy having to pay insurance premiums year after year, but it sure comes in handy if/when there is a break in or a fire.<p>[This message has been edited by Patrick (edited 08-27-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000002||TheFiero64||08-27-2000||03:45 PM||||It is ok to voice what you feel.<BR>Yes, He dose have insurance. We are doing this as a gift of our gratitude to him. A lot of what he lost was hand-me-down stuff that builds memories. The $ from the insurance cannot replace that. By us all pitching in and getting them something will help fill the void. It will be something that there family will hold near to there hearts and pass down through many generations. We are like a second family.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by TheFiero64 (edited 08-27-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000003||Patrick||08-27-2000||04:03 PM||||<BR>I agree that no amount of insurance coverage can replace items of sentimental value. As I earlier stated, my heart goes out to Bob and anyone who has suffered this kind of loss. The most important thing by far is that no one was hurt or killed in the fire. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||TheFiero64||08-27-2000||04:27 PM||||Yes, Thank god that it was just one building and not the house or 3 story barn(fiero candy store).<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mattsfiero||08-27-2000||06:18 PM||||Well it is a shame about what has happened and even though we can't do anything asbout it my hand is raised to Ray and Eric for there words and to Josh for posting it. I will most certainly send something for Bob and Holly without question. They are the reason so many of us have our cars on the road in this area. There hospitality to us has never been anything less then that of a family to them a(Even though this was only my first time going there) I encourage all of you to please help the Club raise the money to hopefully try and get everyhting we can to help them out of this hard time.<P>Thanks Matt||||reg||1
Z||000006||fieroshop||08-27-2000||09:55 PM||||Ray, as a shop owner I feel for the lose of Bob's shop. Even though we have insurance it does not pay for everything, especially for tools amd memorabilia that has been passed down. You can count on me to send you a check. Infact I'll send a check for $50.00. It will be in mail in the AM.<P>------------------<BR> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||6
